<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Round_truncation Q1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A weighing scale gives a very precise reading of \({@val@}\) kg for a sack of maize. The store clerk needs to record this to 1 decimal place.</p>

<p>Two clerks record the value differently:</p>
<p><strong>Clerk A</strong> rounds the value to 1 decimal place.</p>
<p><strong>Clerk B</strong> truncates the value to 1 decimal place (simply cuts off the extra digits, without rounding).</p>

<p><strong>Rounding (Clerk A)</strong></p>

<p>(a) Find the absolute error caused by rounding.</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b) Find the relative error caused by rounding.</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c) Find the percentage error caused by rounding.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>

<p><strong>Truncation (Clerk B)</strong></p>

<p>(d) Find the absolute error caused by truncating.</p>
<p>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p>(e) Find the relative error caused by truncating.</p>
<p>[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>

<p>(f) Find the percentage error caused by truncating.</p>
<p>[[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]] [[feedback:prt6]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The scale gave a precise reading of \({@val@}\) kg.</p>

<p><strong>Rounding (Clerk A):</strong></p>
<p>Rounding \({@val@}\) to 1 decimal place gives \({@rounded@}\) kg.</p>

<p>(a) <strong>Absolute error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Absolute error} = |\text{Rounded value} - \text{Working value}| = |{@rounded@} - {@val@}| = {@ta1@} \text{ kg} \]</p>

<p>(b) <strong>Relative error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Relative error} = \frac{\text{Absolute error}}{\text{Working value}} = \frac{{@ta1@}}{{@val@}} \approx {@ta2@} \]</p>

<p>(c) <strong>Percentage error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Percentage error} = \text{Relative error} \times 100\% = {@ta2@} \times 100\% \approx {@ta3@}\% \]</p>

<p><strong>Truncation (Clerk B):</strong></p>
<p>Truncating \({@val@}\) to 1 decimal place (cutting off the extra digits without rounding) gives \({@truncated@}\) kg.</p>

<p>(d) <strong>Absolute error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Absolute error} = |\text{Truncated value} - \text{Working value}| = |{@truncated@} - {@val@}| = {@ta4@} \text{ kg} \]</p>

<p>(e) <strong>Relative error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Relative error} = \frac{\text{Absolute error}}{\text{Working value}} = \frac{{@ta4@}}{{@val@}} \approx {@ta5@} \]</p>

<p>(f) <strong>Percentage error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Percentage error} = \text{Relative error} \times 100\% = {@ta5@} \times 100\% \approx {@ta6@}\% \]</p>

<p><strong>Comparing the two methods:</strong> Truncation always cuts a value down, so it either matches rounding (when the digit being dropped rounds down anyway) or produces a bigger error than rounding, since rounding can adjust the value up or down to whichever is closer. This is why rounding is generally the more accurate method for everyday measurements.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
